.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt CAPGET 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm capget ,
.Nm capset
.Nd read or give up the calling thread's capabilities
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In linux/capability.h
.Ft int
.Fn capget "cap_user_header_t hdrp" "cap_user_data_t datap"
.Ft int
.Fn capset "cap_user_header_t hdrp" "const cap_user_data_t datap"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Capabilities are the privileges Linux divides the power of user ID 0
into.
This kernel keeps them whole: a thread has every capability or none.
A thread whose effective user ID is 0 has every one, and any other
thread none.
A thread that gives them up with
.Fn capset
has none, whatever its user IDs, and is refused what only a privileged
thread may do, as a thread without the capability is on Linux.
.Pp
The header gives the version of the structures and a thread:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct __user_cap_header_struct {
	uint32_t version;
	int pid;
};

struct __user_cap_data_struct {
	uint32_t effective;
	uint32_t permitted;
	uint32_t inheritable;
};
.Ed
.Pp
.Dv _LINUX_CAPABILITY_VERSION_1 ,
0x19980330, has one data structure, for capabilities 0 to 31.
.Dv _LINUX_CAPABILITY_VERSION_2
and
.Dv _LINUX_CAPABILITY_VERSION_3 ,
0x20071026 and 0x20080522, have two, the second for capabilities 32 to 40.
.Pp
.Fn capget
stores the sets of the thread whose ID the header gives, or of the
caller for 0: every capability in the effective and permitted sets of a
thread that has them, none in those of one that does not, and none in
the inheritable set.
With a null
.Fa datap
it checks the version alone.
.Pp
.Fn capset
changes the caller's sets.
With every set empty, the thread gives up its capabilities.
With the effective and permitted sets whole and the inheritable set
empty, it keeps what it has.
Chromium's zygote gives up the capabilities of each process it forks.
.Pp
A fork passes the state on.
An exec gives a thread that gave up its capabilities every one of them
again when its user IDs make it root, as on Linux, unless no_new_privs
is set
.Pq see Xr prctl 2 ,
the thread has a tracer that is not privileged, or another process
shares its directories; the thread then has none.
An exec that gives them back leaves the new image not dumpable and
clears the parent-death signal, as an exec that changes the effective
user ID does.
A root thread that gave up its capabilities may not attach to another
process that has them, nor open its memory, and a thread that has them
may not make a parent that gave them up its tracer with
.Dv PTRACE_TRACEME .
The check is made at the attach and the open, as on Linux: a tracer
that attached before it gave them up, and a descriptor opened before,
keep working.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Rv -std capget capset
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The header or the data cannot be read or written.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
The version is not one of the three.
The kernel writes
.Dv _LINUX_CAPABILITY_VERSION_3
into the header, and
.Fn capget
with a null
.Fa datap
returns 0.
The pid given to
.Fn capget
is negative.
.Fn capset
was given a set that is neither empty nor whole, or an inheritable
capability.
.It Bq Er EPERM
.Fn capset
was given a pid other than 0 and the caller's, a permitted capability the
thread does not have, or an effective one outside the permitted set.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
The pid given to
.Fn capget
names no thread.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr prctl 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
.Sh STANDARDS
Linux keeps each capability on its own: a partial set and inheritable
capabilities, which it keeps, are refused here.
.Dv PR_SET_KEEPCAPS
and the securebits are not supported.
